    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Narcolepsy without cataplexy due to a medical condition, provisional, improved (G47.429)</content>. PROMIS sleep-related impairment T-score down from 68 to 55, meeting the goal of below 60, with rare unplanned episodes. Blood pressure 124/74 mmHg and pulse 76 on modafinil. [S]<list><item>Continue modafinil 100 mg each morning and two scheduled naps.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic post-traumatic headache, responding to prevention (G44.329)</content>. Headache days down from 54 to 21 in three months, more than the 50 percent reduction targeted. Potassium 4.5 and creatinine 0.88 (eGFR 68) on candesartan 16 mg. [S]<list><item>Continue candesartan 16 mg; consider tapering after 6 to 12 months of good control.</item><item>Continue the headache diary.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Medication-overuse headache, resolved (G44.40)</content>. No NSAIDs since the first visit and acute medicine about once a week. [S]<list><item>Keep acute medicine to no more than 2 days a week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PROMIS sleep-related impairment</td><td>At baseline and after treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>T-score 55, down 13 points from baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Modafinil: blood pressure and heart rate</td><td>Before starting and at follow-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/74 mmHg and pulse 76, against 132/80 and 72 before starting</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Headache days per three months</td><td>2 to 3 months after starting prevention</td><td>Completed today</td><td>21, down from 54</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Basic metabolic panel (potassium and creatinine) on candesartan</td><td>Before starting and a few weeks after; then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Potassium 4.5, creatinine 0.88, eGFR 68, sodium and glucose normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Potassium and creatinine after the candesartan dose increase</td><td>A few weeks after a dose increase</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not drawn after the dose was doubled three months ago, a gap in usual practice for angiotensin receptor blockers. Today&apos;s potassium and creatinine are normal, so no harm resulted. Future dose changes will carry a check in two to four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, heart rate, and weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/74 mmHg, pulse 76, weight 51.5 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Headache diary (headache days and acute-medicine days)</td><td>Daily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; about seven headache days a month</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute headache medicine on no more than 2 days a week</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acetaminophen about once a week; no NSAIDs</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at follow-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Score 2</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Safety advice for sudden sleep episodes</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed; no episodes while standing, walking, or cooking since starting modafinil</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stop daily over-the-counter analgesics and limit acute headache medicine to no more than 2 days a week. Outcome by 2026-05-18: No ibuprofen since the first visit; acetaminophen about once a week [S]</item><item>Cut headache days by at least half by the three-month review. Outcome by 2026-08-17: Headache days fell from 54 to 21 in three months [S]</item><item>Reduce daytime sleep attacks and lower the PROMIS sleep-related impairment T-score below 60. Outcome by 2026-08-17: T-score fell from 68 to 55; one or two planned naps a day and rare unplanned episodes [S]</item><item>Establish whether the spells are epileptic before considering antiseizure medicine. Outcome by 2026-05-18: Typical spells captured on ambulatory EEG were sleep onset; no antiseizure medicine needed [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults (Krumholz A, et al. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713) (2015); Treatment of central disorders of hypersomnolence: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine clinical practice guideline (Maski K, et al. J Clin Sleep Med 2021;17(9):1881-1893) (2021); The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (Cephalalgia 2018;38(1):1-211) (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (Ailani J, et al. Headache 2021;61(7):1021-1039) (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Annual wellness visit in the fall. Continue the headache diary and scheduled naps. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-13: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
